       The principal parts of this interface are

       •   ccuurrsseess__ttrraaccee, which selectively enables different tracing features,
           and

       •   __ttrraacceeff, which writes formatted data to the _t_r_a_c_e file.

           The other functions either return a pointer to a string-area (allo‐
           cated by the corresponding function), or return no value  (such  as
           __ttrraacceedduummpp,  which  implements  the  screen dump for TTRRAACCEE__UUPPDDAATTEE).
           The caller should not free these strings, since the  allocation  is
           reused on successive calls.  To work around the problem of a single
           string-area per  function,  some  use  a  buffer-number  parameter,
           telling the library to allocate additional string-areas.

       The ccuurrsseess__ttrraaccee function is always available, whether or not the other
       trace functions are available:

       •   If tracing is available, calling ccuurrsseess__ttrraaccee with a nonzero param‐
           eter updates the trace mask, and returns the previous trace mask.

           When the trace mask is nonzero, _n_c_u_r_s_e_s creates the file “trace” in
           the current directory for output.  If the file already  exists,  no
           tracing is done.

       •   If tracing is not available, ccuurrsseess__ttrraaccee returns zero (0).

